### Why is @Scheduled single-threaded by default?

Spring creates one thread for all `@Scheduled` tasks. If you have three jobs and one takes an hour, the other two cannot run until it finishes, even though their trigger time arrived. This silently serializes everything.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class BlockingJobs {

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 60_000)
    public void reconciliation() {
        // Takes 40 minutes -- blocks the single thread
        reconciliationService.run();
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 30_000)
    public void heartbeat() {
        // Never runs on time because reconciliation holds the thread
        monitorService.sendHeartbeat();
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 300_000)
    public void cacheRefresh() {
        // Also delayed -- waiting for reconciliation to finish
        cacheService.refresh();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior with single thread:
- 00:00: reconciliation starts, takes 40 min
- 00:01: heartbeat trigger fires -- BLOCKED, waiting
- 00:05: cacheRefresh trigger fires -- BLOCKED, waiting
- 00:40: reconciliation finishes
- 00:40: heartbeat and cacheRefresh finally run (40 min late)
```

### How do you fix the single-thread limitation with a thread pool?

Implement `SchedulingConfigurer` and register a `ThreadPoolTaskScheduler` with multiple threads, or set `spring.task.scheduling.pool.size` in properties.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableScheduling;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.SchedulingConfigurer;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskScheduler;
import org.springframework.scheduling.config.ScheduledTaskRegistrar;

@Configuration
@EnableScheduling
public class SchedulerConfig implements SchedulingConfigurer {

    @Override
    public void configureTasks(ScheduledTaskRegistrar registrar) {
        ThreadPoolTaskScheduler pool = new ThreadPoolTaskScheduler();
        pool.setPoolSize(5);
        pool.setThreadNamePrefix("sched-");
        pool.initialize();
        registrar.setTaskScheduler(pool);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior with 5 threads:
- 00:00: reconciliation starts on sched-1
- 00:01: heartbeat starts on sched-2 (not blocked!)
- 00:05: cacheRefresh starts on sched-3 (not blocked!)
- All three run concurrently
```

### What does no persistence mean for @Scheduled?

Triggers live only in the running JVM's memory. There is no stored job definition, no execution history, and no catch-up. If the application is down at trigger time (deploy, crash, maintenance), that run is simply missed.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ComplianceReport {

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")  // 2 AM daily
    public void generateReport() {
        // If the app is down at 2 AM, this run is MISSED
        // No record, no catch-up, no alert
        reportService.generateDaily();
    }
}
```

```text
Scenario: deployment window from 1:50 AM to 2:15 AM
- 2:00 AM: trigger fires -- app is down
- 2:15 AM: app restarts -- the 2 AM run is lost forever
- Next run: 2:00 AM tomorrow
- Compliance report for today is MISSING
```

### How does Quartz fix the persistence gap?

Quartz with a `JDBCJobStore` stores jobs and triggers in the database. Schedules survive restarts, and misfire handling decides what to do when a trigger was missed while the app was down.

```properties
# application.properties
spring.quartz.job-store-type=jdbc
spring.quartz.properties.org.quartz.jobStore.misfireThreshold=60000
# Misfire policy options: fire now, fire once, do nothing
```

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import org.quartz.Job;
import org.quartz.JobExecutionContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class PersistentComplianceJob implements Job {

    @Override
    public void execute(JobExecutionContext context) {
        // Quartz persists this job in the database
        // If missed at 2 AM, misfire handler runs it on restart
        reportService.generateDaily();
    }
}
```

```text
Scenario with Quartz:
- 2:00 AM: trigger fires -- app is down, trigger stored as misfired
- 2:15 AM: app restarts, Quartz checks for misfires
- 2:15 AM: misfire handler fires the job (fire-now policy)
- Compliance report is generated, just 15 minutes late
```

### Why can you not reschedule @Scheduled at runtime?

The cron string in `@Scheduled(cron = "...")` is bound at startup. To change it you must redeploy. Business often needs to adjust a job's time without a release.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class FixedScheduleJob {

    // This cron is burned in at startup -- no runtime change
    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 22 * * *")  // 10 PM
    public void generateStatements() {
        // Business wants to move this to 8 PM at month-end
        // Requires a code change and redeploy!
    }
}
```

```text
Problem:
- Operations wants to move the job to 8 PM during month-end
- Requires a code change, PR, build, deploy
- Takes hours instead of seconds
```

### How do you implement dynamic scheduling with a programmatic Trigger?

Inject a `TaskScheduler` and schedule a `Runnable` with a `Trigger` whose next execution is computed from a cron value read dynamically from a database or config. Changing the stored value reschedules the job live.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import com.bank.service.CronConfigService;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.TaskScheduler;
import org.springframework.scheduling.support.CronTrigger;
import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;

@Configuration
public class DynamicScheduleConfig {

    private final TaskScheduler taskScheduler;
    private final CronConfigService cronConfig;
    private final Runnable statementJob;

    public DynamicScheduleConfig(TaskScheduler taskScheduler,
                                  CronConfigService cronConfig,
                                  Runnable statementJob) {
        this.taskScheduler = taskScheduler;
        this.cronConfig = cronConfig;
        this.statementJob = statementJob;
    }

    @PostConstruct
    public void schedule() {
        taskScheduler.schedule(statementJob, triggerContext -> {
            // Re-reads cron from DB on every computation
            String cron = cronConfig.getCron("statement-job");
            return new CronTrigger(cron).nextExecution(triggerContext);
        });
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.CronConfigRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class CronConfigService {

    private final CronConfigRepository repo;

    public CronConfigService(CronConfigRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    public String getCron(String jobName) {
        return repo.findByJobName(jobName)
                .map(c -> c.getCronExpression())
                .orElse("0 0 22 * * *"); // default 10 PM
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- Job initially runs at 10 PM (default cron)
- DBA updates cron_config table: statement-job = '0 0 20 * * *'
- Next trigger computation reads new cron
- Job now runs at 8 PM -- no redeploy needed
```

### How do you offload heavy work from the scheduler thread with @Async?

Keep the `@Scheduled` method quick and offload heavy work to an `@Async` method on a separate executor, so the scheduler thread is freed immediately.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Async;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class AsyncScheduledJob {

    private final HeavyWorkService heavyWork;

    public AsyncScheduledJob(HeavyWorkService heavyWork) {
        this.heavyWork = heavyWork;
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 60_000)
    public void trigger() {
        // Quick -- just dispatches the work
        heavyWork.reconcile();
    }
}

@Component
class HeavyWorkService {

    @Async("batchExecutor")
    public void reconcile() {
        // Runs on a separate thread pool -- scheduler thread freed
        // Takes 40 minutes but does not block the scheduler
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- Scheduler thread fires trigger() in milliseconds
- reconcile() runs on batchExecutor thread pool
- Scheduler thread immediately available for other jobs
```

### What is the escalation ladder for scheduling in Spring?

The `@Scheduled` annotation is perfect for simple, fixed, best-effort in-process jobs. The moment you need more, you escalate step by step.

```text
Level 1: @Scheduled (simple, fixed, best-effort)
  Problem: single thread blocks others
  Fix: ThreadPoolTaskScheduler (pool.size = N)

Level 2: ThreadPoolTaskScheduler (concurrent, still in-memory)
  Problem: missed runs when app is down, no history
  Fix: Quartz JDBCJobStore or Kubernetes CronJob

Level 3: Quartz or external scheduler (persistent, misfire handling)
  Problem: schedule fixed at deploy time
  Fix: Programmatic TaskScheduler + dynamic Trigger

Level 4: Dynamic scheduling (runtime reschedule)
  Problem: runs on every instance
  Fix: ShedLock / Quartz clustering / leader election
```

```java
// Level 1: Simple @Scheduled -- good for cache refresh
@Scheduled(fixedRate = 300_000)
public void refreshCache() { cacheService.refresh(); }

// Level 3: Quartz -- good for must-not-miss compliance reports
// Level 4: Dynamic + ShedLock -- good for adjustable production jobs
```

```text
Decision guide:
- Fixed, best-effort, single instance? --> @Scheduled
- Need concurrency? --> Add thread pool
- Must not miss runs? --> Quartz or external scheduler
- Need runtime schedule changes? --> Programmatic dynamic trigger
- Multiple instances? --> ShedLock / Quartz clustering
```
